{{l1_contrast:greetings_pragmatics}}', 'draft'),
  ('1d97fb78-20e3-3416-824d-0dd50a495741', 'a8570b5c-19da-3116-9557-b4ff47172617', 2, 'The Alphabet and Spelling', '[L1: In this lesson you learn the names of the English letters so you can spell your name aloud and understand spelled words. Careful: English letter NAMES are not the same as their sounds, and several names are different from the letters you may know.]
{{l1_contrast:letter_names_vs_sounds}}', 'draft'),
  ('0438384d-08f2-311d-9c40-083380e648db', 'd6e23c47-9ab8-38dd-a703-e27009e9ec30', 3, 'What''s Your Name?', '[L1: In this lesson you learn to ask someone''s name and to give yours with ''I''m…'' and ''My name is…''. You also learn ''Nice to meet you.'' — what you say the first time you meet someone.]
{{l1_contrast:copula_be}}', 'draft'),
  ('777eb3bd-d34f-3239-8642-87e27b8166a2', 'b3967efd-9d80-3745-839e-fa2dcd932b5d', 4, 'Numbers 0–20', '[L1: In this lesson you learn the numbers from 0 to 20 — to say them, to hear them, and to read them in short sentences. The ''-teen'' numbers (13–19) get special attention: they are the ones people mishear most.]', 'draft'),
  ('267866c4-368b-3f42-9f44-4efd5383bbd1', 'd6e23c47-9ab8-38dd-a703-e27009e9ec30', 5, 'Where Are You From?', '[L1: In this lesson you learn to ask and answer "Where are you from?" You will learn country names and the pattern "I''m from ___."]
{{l1_contrast:copula_be}}', 'draft'),
  ('a5123014-0aae-3405-8518-64b6d0c1fae0', 'd6e23c47-9ab8-38dd-a703-e27009e9ec30', 6, 'Nationalities and Languages', '[L1: In this lesson you learn to say your nationality (''I''m Mexican.'') and your languages (''I speak Spanish and English.''). You also learn the question ''Do you speak English?'' — learn it as one fixed phrase; the grammar behind it comes later.]', 'draft'),
  ('a4f53933-4efc-30e8-94a2-674c19dedf68', '302f4854-d7dd-385a-8ddf-8208bb906887', 7, 'be: I am / You are / She is', '[L1: In this lesson you build sentences with am, are and is: I am a student. You are new. She is my teacher. English always needs these small words — you cannot leave them out.]
{{l1_contrast:copula_be}}', 'draft'),
  ('f9948630-7d9d-3976-b23a-17f13c07d907', '302f4854-d7dd-385a-8ddf-8208bb906887', 8, 'be: Negatives and Short Answers', '[L1: In this lesson you say what is NOT true — I''m not a teacher — and give short answers: Yes, I am. / No, I''m not. Careful: short answers end in the full form. Say Yes, I am — never Yes, I''m.]', 'draft'),
  ('22afcd0f-bbe4-3add-aa93-3f08e69b3252', '302f4854-d7dd-385a-8ddf-8208bb906887', 9, 'Are You…? Yes/No Questions with be', '[L1: In this lesson you ask yes/no questions by moving am/is/are to the front: You are new. → Are you new? In these questions your voice goes UP at the end.]', 'draft'),
  ('ee4de8f1-dd22-3abb-a76c-acb1ed3eedd9', '302f4854-d7dd-385a-8ddf-8208bb906887', 10, 'What, Where, Who: WH-Questions with be', '[L1: In this lesson you ask for personal information with what, where and who. You already know two of these questions! In WH-questions your voice goes DOWN at the end: Where are you from?]', 'draft'),
  ('43296b44-a09c-3c84-ada9-af9ada73b4dd', 'b3967efd-9d80-3745-839e-fa2dcd932b5d', 11, 'Numbers 21–100 and Phone Numbers', '[L1: In this lesson you learn the numbers 21–100 and how to say a phone number in English: digit by digit, in small groups. Careful: thirteen and thirty sound very similar — listen for the stress!]', 'draft'),
  ('7ab7e4ce-b4fb-36fc-8bc6-6ef97586f5f0', 'a8570b5c-19da-3116-9557-b4ff47172617', 12, 'Filling In a Form', '[L1: In this lesson you read and complete a simple form in English: first name, last name, country, phone number, email address. Remember: names of people, countries and languages always start with a CAPITAL letter in English.]', 'draft'),
  ('f200cf4a-f9a3-38a4-a94f-2ec57e804427', 'b3967efd-9d80-3745-839e-fa2dcd932b5d', 13, 'Jobs', '[L1: In this lesson you learn ten job words. You say your job with "I''m a…" or "I''m an…" (an before a vowel sound: an engineer, an artist). You also learn the question "What do you do?" — learn it as one piece; the grammar comes later. In English-speaking countries this question is normal, friendly small talk — it is not rude.]
{{l1_contrast:article_a_an_jobs}}', 'draft'),
  ('73990b0c-2fa7-3b7f-acc4-f3ee1cecdeeb', 'b3967efd-9d80-3745-839e-fa2dcd932b5d', 14, 'This Is / That Is: Things Around You', '[L1: In this lesson you learn words for things around you: pen, book, phone, bag, chair, table. You point to a thing NEAR you with "this" and to a thing FAR from you with "that": This is my phone. That is a chair. To ask about a thing, say "What''s this?"]
{{l1_contrast:demonstratives_this_that}}', 'draft'),
  ('5a230171-cce3-33cf-92fa-481d3cc7a4df', 'd6e23c47-9ab8-38dd-a703-e27009e9ec30', 15, 'Survival Phrases: Please, Sorry, Can You Repeat That?', '[L1: In this lesson you learn polite words and "help" phrases. English uses please and thank you very often — more than many languages. "Excuse me" gets attention; "I''m sorry" apologizes; rising "Sorry?" means: say it again. When you don''t understand, you can ALWAYS say: Sorry? · Can you repeat that, please? · How do you spell that? · I don''t understand. Using these phrases is good English, never a failure — they work in every conversation in this course.]
{{l1_contrast:politeness_excuse_vs_sorry}}', 'draft'),
  ('475176be-1386-3175-a702-c24cc2ca324e', '57d4b0a8-4325-3ad0-8d96-07a6c5bf2481', 16, 'Sound Lab 1: ship vs sheep, question melody', '[L1: English has two different vowel sounds that many languages write the same way: the short, quick i in "this" and "six", and the long ee in "see" and "three". Whole words change meaning with this one sound — like "ship" (a boat) and "sheep" (the animal). Today you train your ear with words you already know. You also train question melody: yes/no questions go UP at the end; questions with What and Where go DOWN. The melody tells your listener what kind of answer you want.]
{{l1_contrast:vowel_short_i_vs_long_ee}}', 'draft'),
  ('3f844b10-3e35-30ed-b7f5-ca7abc358264', 'd6e23c47-9ab8-38dd-a703-e27009e9ec30', 17, 'Meet a New Classmate', '[L1: Today you put Unit 1 together: you meet a new classmate. First you rebuild the conversation in parts — greet, names, where from, jobs, goodbye. Then you do it for real with the AI, first in writing at your own speed, then speaking, 6-8 turns. If you don''t understand something, use Sorry? or Can you repeat that, please? — that is always good English and it counts in your favor.]', 'draft'),
  ('5c74ee8c-33ed-3632-8bd2-fb07a150c01b', 'd6e23c47-9ab8-38dd-a703-e27009e9ec30', 18, 'Unit 1 Review + Mission: Three Introductions', '[L1: The last lesson of Unit 1! First, a short review of everything: greetings, names, countries, jobs, numbers, polite phrases. Then your mission — introduce yourself to three different people: a teacher, a student like you, and an office worker. Each one needs different words. Formal: Good morning + Ms/Mr and a last name. Friendly: Hi! and first names. Your repair phrases are always allowed — using them is part of a good score.]
{{l1_contrast:register_formality}}', 'draft')
